  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-8615" textLabel="nucleoside-pyrophosphate kinase 1" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-8615" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="750" centerY="555" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">Nucleoside diphosphate kinase (NDPK) is a ubiquitous housekeeping enzyme that catalyzes the transfer of a &amp;gamma;-phosphoryl group from a nucleoside triphosphate (NTP) to a nucleoside diphosphate (NDP) via a transient phosphorylation of a conserved histidine residue. While all of the eukaryotic NDPKs are homohexamers, some of the prokaryotic enzymes are homotetramers. In plants, NDPKs are involved in responses to heat stress, UV-B light signaling, growth, reactive oxygen species signaling, and phytochrome signaling. |FRAME: TAX-3702| expresses four NDPKs, which have been named NDPK1, NDPK2, NDPK3 and NDPK4. The main difference among the different isoforms involves their regulation. For example. NDPK2 is activated by phytochromes, while NDPK1 is not |CITS: [15465053]|. The NDPK1, NDPK2 and NDPK3 proteins were purified and crystalized, and the crystal structures were reported |CITS: [15465053]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-8615" />
      <Property key="ComplexComponents" value="AT4G09320-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="ComponentCount" value="1" />
      <Property key="IsComplex" value="true" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33848, RXN-7451, RXN-7450, RXN-7449, RXN-7448, RXN-7447, RXN-7446, RXN-7445" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="8" />
      <Property key="^coefficient" value="6" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="CPLX-*" />
      <Property key="Species" value="ORG-5993" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_15465053" />
    </Group>
    <Group elementId="CPLXQT-6" textLabel="ribonucleoside-pyrophosphate reductase" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLXQT-6" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="635" width="230" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Ribonucleotide reductases (RNRs) catalyze the conversion of ribonucleotides to 2&apos;-deoxyribonucleotides which are the precursors for DNA synthesis and repair. These enzymes are divided into three classes, I, II and III. Class I has been divided into classes Ia and Ib and recently class Ic was added (in |CITS: [19492792]|). All three classes share a conserved active site cysteine residue that is converted to a cysteinyl radical which abstracts a hydrogen atom form the ribose ring of the substrate. Classes I, II and III are distinguished by their metal and cofactor requirements and may vary in subunit composition, residues involved in catalysis, dependence on |FRAME: OXYGEN-MOLECULE|, use of NDP and/or NTP substrates, electron source used as reductant, and number of allosteric sites. Class Ia enzymes are found in eukaryotes, eubacteria, bacteriophages and viruses (see the enzymes from |FRAME: CPLX66-276 humans|, |FRAME: CPLXQT-6 &quot;&lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;&quot;| and |FRAME: RIBONUCLEOSIDE-DIP-REDUCTI-CPLX &quot;&lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;|). Class Ib enzymes are found in eubacteria (see |FRAME: RIBONUCLEOSIDE-DIP-REDUCTII-CPLX| from &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;). A |FRAME: CPLX-8116 &quot;class Ic enzyme&quot;| has been identified in |FRAME: TAX-813|. Class II enzymes have been found mostly in archaea and eubacteria (see the |FRAME: CPLX-8111 &quot;enzyme from &lt;i&gt;Thermotoga maritima&lt;/i&gt;&quot;|), although they were also reported from the eukaryotes |FRAME: TAX-3039| and |FRAME: TAX-44689| |CITS: [16368684]|. Class III (anaerobic, oxygen-sensitive) enzymes have been found in archaea, eubacteria and bacteriophages (see |FRAME:RIBONUCLEOSIDE-TRIP-REDUCT-CPLX| from &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;). Reviewed in |CITS: [15158709]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Enzyme&lt;/b&gt; The class Ia ribonucleoside-diphosphate reductase (RDPR) from |FRAME: TAX-3702| catalyzes the conversion of all four ribonucleoside diphosphates to deoxyribonucleoside diphosphates. The active protein is a heterotetramer with an &amp;alpha;2&amp;beta;2 structure. The large subunit is encoded by a single gene (|FRAME: AT2G21790|) in the Arabidopsis genome, while the small subunit is encoded by three genes - |FRAME: AT3G23580| |CITS: [7821432]| , TSO2, and RNR2B |CITS: [16399800]|. The cDNAs encoding both subunits of the |FRAME: TAX-3702| ribonucleotide reductase were isolated, and the proteins were recombinantly expressed in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;. The proteins formed an active holoenzyme and demonstrated ribonucleotide reductase activity |CITS: [Saugemerle97][10542051]|. The small subunit provides a dinuclear non-heme iron center and a stable tyrosyl radical |CITS: [Sauge-Merle97]| while the large subunit provides the substrate-binding site, an activation site, and a specificity site. A mutation in the large subunit (clr8- crinkled leaves 8) causes chloroplast malformation and affects leaf development |CITS: [17346262]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLXQT-6" />
      <Property key="ComplexComponents" value="CPLXQT-11, CPLXQT-12" />
      <Property key="ComponentCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="IsComplex" value="true" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-5056, RXN-7296, RXN-7297, RXN-7298, RXN-7300" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="5" />
      <Property key="^coefficient" value="1, 1" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="CPLX-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_SAUGEMERLE97" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_10542051" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_17346262" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_19492792" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_16368684" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_15158709" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_7821432" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_16399800" />
    </Group>
    <Group elementId="CPLXQT-13" textLabel="CPLXQT-13" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLXQT-13" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="750" centerY="715" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment>The cDNA encoding the small subunit (R2) of ribonucleotide reductase (RNR) has been identified and characterized in &lt;i&gt;Arabiodpsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. The gene (At5g40942, RNR2B) belongs to a small gene family of three members which encode for the small subunit of RNR. Mutation in the RNR2B gene resulted in reduced deoxynucleoside triphosphate levels (dNTP&apos;s) and developmental defects but was not lethal which has been discussed as a consequence of the presence of two more redundant genes (RNR2A, TSO2) encoding for the small subunit or RNR |CITS: [16399800]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLXQT-13" />
      <Property key="ComplexComponents" value="AT5G40942-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="ComponentCount" value="1" />
      <Property key="IsComplex" value="true" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-6395, RXN-7608, RXN-7609, RXN-7610" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="4" />
      <Property key="^coefficient" value="2" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="CPLX-*" />
      <Property key="Species" value="ARA" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_16399800" />
    </Group>
  </Groups>
